---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import CommandTable from '../components/CommandTable.astro';
import WorkflowDiagram from '../components/WorkflowDiagram.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';

const frontmatterFields = [
  { command: 'to', description: 'Recipient address(es). Required. Comma-separated for multiple.' },
  { command: 'cc', description: 'Carbon copy address(es). Optional.' },
  { command: 'bcc', description: 'Blind carbon copy address(es). Optional.' },
  { command: 'subject', description: 'Email subject line. Required.' },
  { command: 'status', description: 'Workflow state: draft, approved, or sent. Required.' },
  { command: 'from', description: 'Sender address. Optional -- overrides default_from from config.' },
  { command: 'reply_to', description: 'Reply-to address. Optional.' },
  { command: 'attachments', description: 'List of file paths to attach; a folder path attaches every file inside it. Optional.' },
  { command: 'in_reply_to', description: 'Message-ID of the message this draft answers. Written by mp reply; on send, the source is marked answered.' },
  { command: 'forwarded_from', description: 'Message-ID of the message this draft forwards. Written by mp forward; on send, the source is marked forwarded.' },
];

const basicDraft = `---
to: recipient@example.com
cc: optional@example.com
bcc: hidden@example.com
subject: "Your subject line"
status: draft
from: alternate@example.com
reply_to: reply@example.com
attachments:
  - /path/to/file.pdf
---

Email body in **Markdown** format.`;

const replyExample = `---
to: original-sender@example.com
subject: "Re: Original subject"
status: draft
in_reply_to: "<message-id@example.com>"
---

Your reply goes here.

-- 
Your Name

{{SIGNATURE}}

> On 2026-01-15, sender wrote:
>
> Original message text quoted here.`;

const forwardExample = `---
to: ""
subject: "Fwd: Original subject"
status: draft
forwarded_from: "<message-id@example.com>"
attachments:
  - /path/to/original-attachment.pdf
---

Your note to the recipient.

-- 
Your Name

{{SIGNATURE}}

---------- Forwarded message ----------
From: Original Sender <sender@example.com>
Date: 2026-01-15
Subject: Original subject
To: you@example.com

Original message body here.`;

const signatureExample = `[signatures]
default = "work"

[signatures.work]
name = "Work Signature"
text = """
-- 
**Your Name**
Title | Organization
your.name@example.com
"""`;

const attachmentStructure = `accounts/<name>/attachments/<message-id>/
  document.pdf
  image.png`;

const attachmentFrontmatter = `---
to: recipient@example.com
subject: "Fwd: Document delivery"
status: draft
attachments:
  - "/home/you/.local/share/mailypoppins/accounts/work/attachments/abc@example.com/document.pdf"
  - "/home/you/Documents/cover-letter.pdf"
---`;

const sentExample = `---
to: recipient@example.com
subject: "Project update"
status: sent
sent_at: "2026-04-22T10:30:00Z"
sent_via: "mailypoppins 0.6.0"
message_id: "<unique-id@example.com>"
---

Email body here.`;
---

<Layout title="Draft Format -- mailypoppins" description="Markdown email drafts with YAML frontmatter -- fields, status workflow, attachments, and signatures.">
  <Nav />
  <main id="main-content" class="content page">
    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>Draft Format</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Markdown email drafts with YAML frontmatter.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2 id="basic-format">Basic Format</h2>
      <p>
        Email drafts are plain Markdown files with YAML frontmatter. The frontmatter holds
        metadata (recipients, subject, status), and the body below the second <code>---</code>
        is the email content written in Markdown, which gets converted to HTML when sent.
      </p>
      <Code code={basicDraft} lang="markdown" theme="catppuccin-mocha" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="frontmatter-fields">Frontmatter Fields</h2>
      <CommandTable commands={frontmatterFields} />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="status-workflow">Status Workflow</h2>
      <p>
        Drafts follow a strict workflow. Only approved emails can be sent -- the <code>send</code>
        command rejects drafts that have not been approved first. These three are the whole
        vocabulary of <code>status:</code>; where a message sits (inbox, archive, sent) is a
        mailbox, not a status.
      </p>
      <WorkflowDiagram />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="after-sending">After Sending</h2>
      <p>
        When an email is sent, the file is updated in-place. The <code>status</code> changes
        to <code>sent</code>, and three fields are added: <code>sent_at</code> (ISO 8601 timestamp),
        <code>sent_via</code> (tool version), and <code>message_id</code> (the RFC 822 Message-ID,
        used for sync deduplication).
      </p>
      <Code code={sentExample} lang="markdown" theme="catppuccin-mocha" />
      <p>
        A send that reached every recipient and was recorded in the outbox then deletes
        the draft file: the copy that matters lives on the server, where the outbox appends
        it to Sent and sync reads it back into the local store.
      </p>
      <p>
        A partial send, or one the outbox store could not record, keeps the file with
        <code>status: sent</code>, because it is the only local copy left. Sending it again means
        editing <code>status:</code> back to <code>approved</code> by hand, because
        <code>mp send</code> refuses anything that is not approved and <code>mp mark-approved</code>
        refuses anything already sent. Trim the recipient lines first: a re-send delivers to
        everyone the file still lists, including the ones who already received it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The raw RFC 822 message is also appended to the server-side Sent folder
        via IMAP APPEND with the <code>\Seen</code> flag. This is best-effort -- a failed
        APPEND is logged but does not fail the send.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="reply-drafts">Reply Drafts</h2>
      <p>
        Reply drafts are created with <code>mp reply</code> (or <code>r</code> to reply,
        <code>ca</code> to reply-all, in the TUI). They record the source message in <code>in_reply_to</code>. The account signature
        (if configured) is appended to the reply area, above a <code>{"{{SIGNATURE}}"}</code>
        placeholder that marks the boundary with the quoted original message.
      </p>
      <p>
        When the reply is sent, the message it answers is marked as answered, locally and
        on the server. The list then shows a reply arrow next to it instead of the plain
        read marker.
      </p>
      <p>
        The signature is part of the draft body, so it is visible and editable before you
        send. The <code>{"{{SIGNATURE}}"}</code> placeholder carries no signature text of its
        own; the send path uses it to place the quoted original below your reply. If it is
        removed, the quote falls back to the end of the body.
      </p>
      <Code code={replyExample} lang="markdown" theme="catppuccin-mocha" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="forward-drafts">Forward Drafts</h2>
      <p>
        Forward drafts are created with <code>mp forward</code> (or <code>cf</code> in the TUI).
        The <code>to:</code> field is left empty for you to fill in. The subject gets a
        "Fwd:" prefix, and any attachments from the original email are listed in the
        <code>attachments:</code> array with their absolute file paths. The source message
        is recorded in <code>forwarded_from</code> and is marked as forwarded once the draft
        goes out.
      </p>
      <Code code={forwardExample} lang="markdown" theme="catppuccin-mocha" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="signatures">Signatures</h2>
      <p>
        Signatures are Markdown, defined per account in <code>config.toml</code> under the
        <code>[signatures]</code> section. A default signature name is set with
        <code>signatures.default</code>; each named entry supplies the snippet inline with
        <code>text</code> or by a <code>path</code> to a Markdown file (<code>~</code> expansion
        supported, <code>text</code> wins when both are present).
      </p>
      <p>
        The signature is appended to the draft body when the draft is created: after the
        body for a new message, in the reply area above the quoted content for a reply or
        forward. An account with no configured signature adds nothing.
      </p>
      <Code code={signatureExample} lang="toml" theme="catppuccin-mocha" />
      <p>
        Use <code>-s &lt;name&gt;</code> to select a specific signature, or
        <code>--no-signature</code> to skip it entirely.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="attachments">Attachments</h2>
      <p>
        A draft's <code>attachments:</code> field holds file paths, and any readable path works.
        An entry that points at a folder attaches every file directly inside it (sorted by name,
        subfolders and dotfiles skipped), so a batch of files can be named by one directory rather
        than listed one by one.
        Received attachments are not files: they live as blobs beside the message row in the
        account's store. Forwarding a message therefore materialises its attachments once, into a
        stable per-message directory under the account, and writes those paths into the draft, so
        the forward still resolves them after the original has been archived.
      </p>
      <Code code={attachmentStructure} lang="text" theme="catppuccin-mocha" />
      <Code code={attachmentFrontmatter} lang="yaml" theme="catppuccin-mocha" />
      <p>
        To read an attachment of a received message rather than forward it: in the TUI press
        <code>to</code> to open one in the default app (it is written to a private temp directory
        first) or <code>ts</code> to save it to a directory you choose, and from the CLI use
        <code>mp save &lt;selector&gt; --output &lt;dir&gt;</code>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-top: var(--space-16);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-16);
  }

  .page-header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
  }

  .subtitle {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--color-subtext0);
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
</style>
